edau et al.", "year": 2024, "version": "Crossref DOI record and deposited abstract; publisher full text not retrieved", "kind": "source", "status": "abstract-only", "readDepth": "Deposited abstract and DOI/title/publication identity read; not full paper.", "sectionsRead": ["Deposited abstract", "DOI/title metadata"], "claim": "Reports resonant laser excitation of Th229 in doped CaF2 and a Th232 control without the corresponding fluorescence signal.", "limitations": "The initially guessed arXiv2404.02939 was a different paper and is retained privately as an identity mismatch; no inference uses it. Experimental methods and full uncertainty not independently reviewed.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The abstract reports an isotope-control discrimination and resonant fluorescence; this is not a clock accuracy certification.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/frequency/freq-thorium-laser-record.json", "sha256": "88ec55221a8cce36d656c22501a3822aaa39f5cc47c169e7e7a3f0e7b927ca6f", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:26:25.443461+00:00", "doi": "10.1103/PhysRevLett.132.182501", "domain": "frequency", "recordKind": "primary-paper", "sourceKind": "primary-paper"}</data></node><node id="freq-codata-2022"><data key="title">NIST 2022 CODATA recommended values: complete ASCII table</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "freq-codata-2022", "title": "NIST 2022 CODATA recommended values: complete ASCII table", "url": "https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Constants/Table/allascii.txt", "authors": "NIST / CODATA", "year": 2022, "version": "2022 adjustment; shared editorial capture inspected 2026-11-25", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Actual shared raw-table rows read; an independent repeat HTTP request returned403 and is not claimed successful.", "sectionsRead": ["h,c,e,k_B defining-value rows", "alpha,mu_0,epsilon_0 measured-value/uncertainty rows"], "claim": "h,c,e,k_B are exact defining values; mu0 and epsilon0 retain measured uncertainties in the revised SI.", "limitations": "Raw capture was retrieved by the moderator and reused with its exact source timestamp/hash. This review does not independently re-adjust the experimental constants.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "The historical value mu0=4π×10^−7 N/A² is no longer exact; precision examples must retain CODATA uncertainty.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/frequency/codata-2022-r2.txt", "sha256": "77fb90e66c40db3e6eb16630bc9c88e4c7c8beddbe5e71be406f2f26e3f67e67", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:27:01.837771+00:00", "domain": "frequency", "recordKind": "official-data", "sourceKind": "official-data"}</data></node><node id="freq-thorium-ratio-2024-review"><data key="title">Frequency ratio of the 229mTh nuclear isomeric transition and the 87Sr atomic clock</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">abstract-only</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "freq-thorium-ratio-2024-review", "sourceId": "freq-thorium-ratio-2024", "topicIds": ["freq-spectroscopy"], "question": "What does this source actually measure or demonstrate, and which inference remains unsupported?", "method": "Reports VUV-comb linkage of thorium nuclear and strontium electronic transitions, with a frequency-ratio measurement.", "result": "Reports VUV-comb linkage of thorium nuclear and strontium electronic transitions, with a frequency-ratio measurement.", "whatIsNew": "Source authors’ specific experimental advance as summarized in the inspected sections; independent novelty certification not performed.", "limitations": "Publisher page explicitly exposes subscription preview only; methods, full paper and experimental data were not read.", "status": "abstract-only", "equationsUsed": ["freq-photon", "freq-comb"], "replication": "No laboratory reproduction, raw-data reanalysis or independent uncertainty-budget reconstruction performed.", "nextQuestions": ["Which systematic shifts and correlations limit the claimed observable?", "What external replication or independently reproducible data would test this result?"], "title": "Frequency ratio of the 229mTh nuclear isomeric transition and the 87Sr atomic clock", "kind": "paper", "domain": "frequency"}</data></node><node id="freq-nuclear-feedback-2026-review"><data key="title">A thorium-229 optical nuclear clock with feedback loop</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">selected-body-sections-reviewed</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "freq-nuclear-feedback-2026-review", "sourceId": "freq-nuclear-feedback-2026", "topicIds": ["freq-spectroscopy"], "question": "What does this source actually measure or demonstrate, and which inference remains unsupported?", "method": "Absorption error-signal locking with20s or30min integration; overlapping Allan comparison against a Yb+ reference and crystal-position scans.", "result": "Clock-performance body reports low10^−14 within a run but about5×10^−13 between-day reproducibility; position-dependent shifts reach1.7kHz.", "whatIsNew": "Source reports a working nuclear-transition feedback loop, not merely excitation; spatial reproducibility and host inhomogeneity remain measured limitations.", "limitations": "Clock performance section reports shifts up to1.7kHz between probed crystal regions; host strain is a proposed explanation. Improved future linewidth/power projections are not demonstrated clock accuracy. No independent systematic-budget reconstruction.", "status": "selected-body-sections-reviewed", "equationsUsed": ["freq-photon", "freq-comb"], "replication": "No laboratory reproduction, raw-data reanalysis or independent uncertainty-budget reconstruction performed.", "nextQuestions": ["Can reproducible optical paths and host-strain control remove the between-day shifts?", "Which full systematic shifts limit an absolute frequency claim independently of Allan stability?"], "title": "A thorium-229 optical nuclear clock with feedback loop", "kind": "paper", "domain": "frequency"}</data></node><node id="freq-optical-rabi-2026-review"><data key="title">Spatially defined Rabi spectroscopy for uninterrupted optical clock interrogation</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">selected-body-sections-reviewed</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "freq-optical-rabi-2026-review", "sourceId": "freq-optical-rabi-2026", "topicIds": ["freq-clock-reference"], "question": "What does this source actually measure or demonstrate, and which inference remains unsupported?", "method": "Moving-lattice Sr stream with a magnetic-field-defined12mm excitation region; compare spectrum with finite-time Fourier model, include light/dephasing and approximate BBR estimates.", "result": "Transit time0.75s gives rectangular-window power FWHM about0.886/0.75=1.1813Hz, consistent with reported1.2Hz. This is interrogation spectroscopy, not a full new-clock accuracy evaluation.", "whatIsNew": "Spatially defined continuous interrogation separates preparation/detection from interaction; the next feedback/stability step remains conditional.", "limitations": "A finite-time Rabi excitation line is not a Lorentzian intrinsic lifetime. Discussion treats dead-time-free closed-loop stabilization as future work; BBR estimate uses approximate emissivity/area weighting and later thermometry, not a complete simultaneous accuracy certification.", "status": "selected-body-sections-reviewed", "equationsUsed": ["freq-fractional", "freq-rectangular-width"], "replication": "No laboratory reproduction, raw-data reanalysis or independent uncertainty-budget reconstruction performed.", "nextQuestions": ["What stability does a demonstrated closed loop achieve once transport delay and finite atomic response are included?", "How do view-factor/temperature and stray-light uncertainty limit the transition-centre evaluation?"], "title": "Spatially defined Rabi spectroscopy for uninterrupted optical clock interrogation", "kind": "paper", "domain": "frequency"}</data></node><node id="freq-comb-ranging-2026-review"><data key="title">A Ranging System Integrating Time‐of‐Flight Approach With Optical‐Frequency‐Comb Interferometry for Ultra‐Precise Long‐Distance Measurements</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "freq-comb-ranging-2026-review", "sourceId": "freq-comb-ranging-2026", "topicIds": ["freq-periodic-combs"], "question": "What does this source actually measure or demonstrate, and which inference remains unsupported?", "method": "Combines coarse time-of-flight and fine comb interferometry for absolute ranging.", "result": "Combines coarse time-of-flight and fine comb interferometry for absolute ranging.", "whatIsNew": "Source authors’ specific experimental advance as summarized in the inspected sections; independent novelty certification not performed.", "limitations": "Reported apparatus-specific distance errors are not independently reanalysed, and air-index/reference uncertainties are not resolved by citing the comb equation.", "status": "selected-text", "equationsUsed": ["freq-comb"], "replication": "No laboratory reproduction, raw-data reanalysis or independent uncertainty-budget reconstruction performed.", "nextQuestions": ["Which systematic shifts and correlations limit the claimed observable?", "What external replication or independently reproducible data would test this result?"], "title": "A Ranging System Integrating Time‐of‐Flight Approach With Optical‐Frequency‐Comb Interferometry for Ultra‐Precise Long‐Distance Measurements", "kind": "paper", "domain": "frequency"}</data></node><node id="freq-floquet-2026-review"><data key="title">Chirality-selective second-harmonic response with Floquet engineering</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">frequency</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><d